    <h1>Raven the Trickster: Northwest Coast Indigenous Traditions</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian, and other Northwest Coast Indigenous traditions, Raven (Yéil / Yáahl) is the supreme Trickster-Creator. He steals the sun, moon, and stars from a powerful chief and releases them for humanity. He frees the first people from a giant clamshell. He transforms the landscape. The Raven moiety is one of two fundamental social divisions in Tlingit society — roughly half of all Tlingit people are Ravens. This tradition represents one of the world's richest and most complex raven mythologies.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Who Is Raven?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In Northwest Coast tradition, Raven is not simply a clever bird. He is:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A transformer:</strong> before Raven's actions, the world was dark, formless, or incomplete. Raven transforms it — bringing light, creating rivers and mountains, releasing animals and people — through his actions.</li>
      <li><strong>A trickster:</strong> Raven acts from desire (usually hunger or curiosity) rather than benevolent intent. His great gifts to humanity (light, fresh water, fire) are often accidentally or reluctantly given — byproducts of his schemes for personal gain. This moral ambiguity is fundamental to the tradition.</li>
      <li><strong>A shape-shifter:</strong> Raven can transform into human form or other animals at will. Many stories involve him adopting disguises to get what he wants.</li>
      <li><strong>An ancestor:</strong> in some Northwest Coast traditions, Raven is a clan ancestor — the first being of the Raven moiety — not just a mythological figure.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Steals the Sun</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The most widely told story in the Raven cycle describes how Raven brought light to the world:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>At the beginning of time, the world is dark. A powerful man (sometimes identified as the grandfather of all beings) keeps the sun, moon, and stars locked in nested boxes in his house. No one can get near them.</li>
      <li>Raven, greedy and determined, transforms himself into a spruce needle floating in a stream. The man's daughter drinks from the stream and swallows the needle. She becomes pregnant and gives birth to a child — Raven in disguise.</li>
      <li>The "child" (Raven) cries for the boxes. The grandfather eventually relents, letting the child play with the boxes one by one. Raven opens the box containing the stars, then the moon, then the sun — releasing them into the sky — and flies away through the smoke-hole.</li>
      <li>As Raven escapes through the smoke, the soot blackens his feathers permanently — explaining why the raven is black.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven and the First Humans</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In a Haida version of Raven mythology, Raven walks along the beach after the great flood. He hears tiny voices and discovers the first humans trapped inside a giant clamshell, afraid to come out into the world. Raven coaxes, teases, and eventually tricks them into emerging — and from this moment, humanity begins.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>This version is familiar to many people through Bill Reid's monumental sculpture <em>The Raven and the First Men</em> (1980), displayed at the Museum of Anthropology at the University of British Columbia — perhaps the most celebrated artwork of the Northwest Coast tradition.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven Moiety in Tlingit Society</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Among the Tlingit, the raven is not just a mythological figure but a living social institution:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Tlingit society is divided into two halves (moieties) — Raven (Yéil) and Eagle (Ch'áak'). Every Tlingit person belongs to one moiety, inherited matrilineally.</li>
      <li>Moiety membership regulates marriage (you must marry someone from the opposite moiety), ceremony (the opposite moiety performs funerary and potlatch work for you), and social obligation.</li>
      <li>Roughly half the Tlingit people are Ravens — meaning the raven moiety encompasses approximately 15,000–20,000 people today.</li>
      <li>Within each moiety, there are many clans (sib groups), each with their own crests and histories.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven in Northwest Coast Art</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Raven is ubiquitous in Northwest Coast art — on totem poles, house screens, bentwood boxes, button blankets, and contemporary works. The raven is typically depicted with its characteristic hooked bill, often shown in its transforming state (part raven, part human) or carrying the sun in its beak. The formal conventions of Northwest Coast two-dimensional art (formline design, ovoid shapes, U-forms) encode Raven's stories in visual languages with their own grammar, as analysed by Bill Holm (1965).<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

